[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". The reports merged into it say
otherwise. Grouped by what can actually break a date for that tenant, not by subject line, they
fall into three problems. Each needs its own fix, so each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix closed only the first problem. Dates that move by one day are still broken.
</Callout>

## Where they stand

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October:

- `parser` — **fixed** ("fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales"). [#1](https://github.com/yaluke/mega-what-it-shows/issues/1) closed as completed.
- `hint` — **won't fix**: the `booking-v3` form is retired. [#2](https://github.com/yaluke/mega-what-it-shows/issues/2) closed as not planned.
- `one-day` — **still open.** Neither outcome touches it: a day-first parse cannot move a date back by one day, and these reports came through the app and email, not the retired form. [#3](https://github.com/yaluke/mega-what-it-shows/issues/3) stays open.

## Where the reports go

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

The export repeats some reports across overlapping pages. Each report is counted once, by id.

## The three problems

**`parser` — the shared parser reads every date month first.** Every tenant on file writes the
day first, so any day ≤ 12 comes out with day and month swapped: 7 April becomes 4 July. It
reaches people through the app, email, and the `booking-v2` form. This is the fix that is queued.

**`hint` — `booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** Day-first tenants on
`booking-v3` type what the hint tells them, month first, and the form then reads the date the
tenant's way. The symptom is the same swap, so subject lines and intake tags cannot tell it from
`parser`; only the channel and the tenant's form can. Fixing the parser does not touch the hint.

**`one-day` — dates move by exactly one day.** "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday
11 May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." A month-first read cannot
move a date by one day, so these are not `parser` at all. They point at time zones: a date
handled as midnight in one zone and shown in another. The desk had already noticed them
(`desk-notes.json`) but counted them under `parser`.

## How to tell them apart

- Ignore `meta.intakeTag`. It is a guess from the reporter's browser and disagrees with the
  tenant-based cause on dozens of these reports.
- Day and month swapped, reported through a `booking-v3` form → `hint`. Any other way in → `parser`.
- The date moved by one day, or two people saw different weekdays for one meeting → `one-day`,
  whatever the tenant.

